WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.710
The power went off for the whole

2
00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:05.330
of exam week, and my computing

3
00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:06.990
class still happened.

4
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.280
Every pupil had a printed sheet:

5
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.160
lines to put in order, a table to

6
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.580
fill in, a bug to circle.

7
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.900
We learned as much as with the phones.

8
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:22.860
Today: teaching and

9
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.480
assessing code on paper.

10
00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:27.950
Start with Parsons strips.

11
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.360
Print a short program, cut it into

12
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.720
lines, and add one distractor.

13
00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.690
Pairs put the strips in order on the desk,

14
00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:39.010
and draw the indents with a pencil.

15
00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.170
You can check twenty desks in two minutes.

16
00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.240
Next, trace tables.

17
00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.710
Pupils copy a short program and draw

18
00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.250
a column for each variable.

19
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.920
They go line by line, writing

20
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.960
each new value in a new row.

21
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.030
The last row shows what

22
00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:57.910
the program prints.

23
00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:02.090
A desk check is a trace table for your own

24
00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.190
code or a partner’s, before you run it.

25
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.520
Pupils write a program on paper, then

26
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.320
trace it with two test inputs.

27
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.200
If the table disagrees with what

28
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.420
they expected, there’s a bug to

29
00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.580
find, with no device at all.

30
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.350
Paper is also a fair way to assess.

31
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.740
Predict-the-output questions, Parsons

32
00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.380
strips and find-the-bug questions test

33
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.340
understanding, not typing speed.

34
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.180
Every pupil gets the same chance,

35
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.180
with or without a phone at home.

36
00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:35.860
Quick check!

37
00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:37.670
Trace it in your head.

38
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.940
n starts at one and total at zero;

39
00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:46.920
while n is at most three, add n to

40
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.740
total and add one to n.

41
00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:51.170
What does it print?

42
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:52.900
Six.

43
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.320
A trace table makes it visible: one

44
00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.860
row each time round the loop, with n

45
00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.640
and total side by side.

46
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.960
Pupils who answered ten forgot that the

47
00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.300
loop stops when n reaches four.

48
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.040
You don’t have to make all

49
00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:09.820
this from scratch.

50
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:12.530
Every Darsi lesson has a

51
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:13.310
printable worksheet.

52
00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.510
Its code questions come out as paper

53
00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:19.750
exercises: strips to number, gaps to

54
00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.830
fill, a line to circle, and the robot

55
00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:24.090
grid with cut-out cards.

56
00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.890
Paper and phones work best together.

57
00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:31.960
Pupils predict and trace on paper, then

58
00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.400
one phone per group checks the answer.

59
00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:37.280
The phone becomes the judge, and

60
00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.760
the thinking stays on paper,

61
00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.380
where everyone can do it.

62
00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:44.340
In the app, open Step through a program.

63
00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.060
Fill in a trace table on paper

64
00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.280
for A loop that adds, then press

65
00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.140
Next and check each row.

66
00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.300
Then change range of one, five to

67
00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:59.140
range of one, eleven, and predict

68
00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:00.780
the total before you run it.

69
00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:02.590
Your turn.

70
00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:06.630
Half your class has no phone at home.

71
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.960
Which homework is fairest?

72
00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:11.470
Paper.

73
00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.810
Remember: Parsons strips, trace

74
00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.170
tables and desk checks teach real

75
00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:18.790
programming with no device.

76
00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.770
Paper is a fair way to assess.

77
00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.320
Darsi’s worksheets print the

78
00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:25.360
code steps for you.

79
00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.400
And one phone can be the judge.

80
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.460
Try it in class tomorrow: a

81
00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:31.940
paper-only lesson.

82
00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.380
Print the worksheet from this lesson’s

83
00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.480
kit, run it with pairs, and use one phone

84
00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.300
